<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Septuagint</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">3rd–2nd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as LXX</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Septuagint is the earliest Greek translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> scriptures, begun with the Torah in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in the third century BCE and completed over the following two centuries. The Letter of Aristeas tells that seventy-two scholars made it at the request of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-ii-philadelphus">Ptolemy II Philadelphus</a>. It became the Bible of Greek-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and of the early Church and remains the Old Testament of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/septuagint.jpg" alt="Septuagint" width="320" height="561" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Codex_Vaticanus_(1_Esdras_1-55_to_2-5)_(The_S.S._Teacher%27s_Edition-The_Holy_Bible).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
